TEXT -416 -288 Left 3 ;#412 Common-emitter amplifier - bypassing the emitter resistor
TEXT -416 -232 Left 2 ;Source: Alan Wolke (W2AEW), https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yhuf2_Zu8T8
TEXT -416 -184 Left 2 ;CEMIT removes the emitter degeneration at 1 kHz, so the gain is set by RC over the transistor's own small-signal emitter resistance.
TEXT 944 -128 Left 2 !.step param Cbyp list 4.7u 100u
TEXT 944 -80 Left 2 !.tran 0 40m 36m 1u
TEXT 944 -32 Left 2 !.meas tran CH1_PP PP V(base) FROM 36m TO 40m
TEXT 944 16 Left 2 !.meas tran CH2_PP PP V(collector) FROM 36m TO 40m
TEXT 944 64 Left 2 !.meas tran GAIN PARAM CH2_PP/CH1_PP
TEXT 944 144 Left 2 ;4.7u is the value on Alan's notes. At 1 kHz its 33.9 ohm reactance is larger than the
TEXT 944 176 Left 2 ;transistor's 26 ohm small-signal emitter resistance, so it only partly bypasses RE.
TEXT 944 224 Left 2 ;100u is a modelling choice: 1.6 ohm at 1 kHz, low enough to be a short across RE.
TEXT -416 432 Left 2 ;RSER 76.5k is the middle of the 76k to 77k Alan dials in so his 700 mV generator leaves about 18 mV at the base.
TEXT -416 480 Left 2 ;RLOAD 1Meg stands in for the open output terminal, which LTspice needs for a DC path.
